From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:38:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: isp: park the T8132 ISP cores at
 power-down by default

Turn core_park on by default. It only acts on hardware that names the
core power words, which is T8132 alone.

The sequence was first taken from a stop where the firmware never got
POWER_DOWN. macOS 26B123 on the J604, traced again with the ANE
present, ends both ISP sessions it runs at boot with POWER_DOWN
completed (res 0) and then writes the same words in the same order:
bits 24-25 of coprocessor word 0xc00 cleared, the four fabric words
cleared, the quiesce, and 2 to the two core power words, which read
back 6. So this is how macOS stops the ISP on every clean stop.

core_park=0 restores the quiesce alone.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c | 7 +++----
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c b/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c
index 766de95181..ba69ee8d89 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c
@@ -44,13 +44,12 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(macos_irq_enables,
 
 /*
  * Park the ISP CPU cores at power-down in macOS's order where that is
- * known (T8132). The camera stops and starts again without it; off until
- * measured.
+ * known (T8132): macOS 26B123 writes the same words on every clean stop.
  */
-static bool core_park;
+static bool core_park = true;
 module_param(core_park, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(core_park,
-		 "Park the ISP CPU cores before power-down as macOS does, e.g. T8132 (default: off)");
+		 "Park the ISP CPU cores before power-down as macOS does, e.g. T8132 (default: on)");
 
 /* the waits for firmware that has been told to power down */
 #define ISP_STOP_KEY_TRIES     20
